      "worked": "Let $p=\\log_a b$, $q=\\log_b c$ and $r=\\log_c a$, as suggested. The logarithm bases are positive and different from $1$.\n\nBy change of base,\n\n$$\\begin{align*}pqr&=\\frac{\\ln b}{\\ln a}\\times\\frac{\\ln c}{\\ln b}\\times\\frac{\\ln a}{\\ln c}\\\\pqr&=1.\\end{align*}$$\n\nThe product law and change of base give\n\n$$\\begin{align*}x&=\\log_a b+\\log_a c\\\\x&=p+pq\\\\x&=p(1+q).\\end{align*}$$\n\nSimilarly, $y=q(1+r)$ and $z=r(1+p)$. Thus\n\n$$\\begin{align*}xyz&=pqr(1+q)(1+r)(1+p)\\\\xyz&=(1+p)(1+q)(1+r)\\\\xyz&=1+p+q+r+pq+qr+rp+pqr\\\\xyz&=2+p+q+r+pq+qr+rp.\\end{align*}$$\n\nAlso,\n\n$$\\begin{align*}x+y+z+2&=p(1+q)+q(1+r)+r(1+p)+2\\\\x+y+z+2&=2+p+q+r+pq+qr+rp.\\end{align*}$$\n\nThe expressions agree, so $xyz=x+y+z+2$.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
